---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { manifest, summary } from "../data/manifest-loader";

const s = summary();
const csvShort = manifest.csv_sha256.slice(0, 12);
const csvFull = manifest.csv_sha256;
const fetchedISO = manifest.fetched_at;
const fetchedDate = new Date(fetchedISO).toISOString().slice(0, 10);
---

<Base
  title="METHODOLOGY — PURSUE://INDEX"
  description="How the pursueindex corpus is fetched, hashed, OCR'd, and made reproducible."
  active="methodology"
>
  <div class="space-y-6 max-w-3xl">
    <header class="space-y-2 border-b border-[color:var(--color-border)] pb-4">
      <div class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[color:var(--color-signal-green)] flex items-center gap-2">
        <span class="text-[color:var(--color-text-faint)]">$</span>
        <span>man pursue-index</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-2xl font-mono font-semibold tracking-tight text-[color:var(--color-text-bright)]">
        METHODOLOGY
      </h1>
      <p class="text-sm text-[color:var(--color-text-dim)] max-w-2xl">
        Every claim on this site traces back to a specific page of a specific
        document. This page documents how that pipeline works, end to end.
      </p>
    </header>

    {/* Source provenance */}
    <section id="provenance" class="border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-bg)]/60 p-5 pi-bracket relative scanlines-soft space-y-3 scroll-mt-20">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-[12px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[color:var(--color-signal-green)]">
        ▸ Source provenance
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
        The U.S. Department of War publishes the PURSUE corpus as a single
        CSV at
        <code class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)] break-all">war.gov/Portals/1/Interactive/2026/UFO/uap-csv.csv</code>.
        Every fetch snapshots that file, archives the raw bytes, and pins
        the SHA-256 in the manifest. If the upstream CSV changes — a new
        tranche, a corrected row, a withdrawn entry — the next snapshot
        produces a different hash and the diff page surfaces every
        per-card delta.
      </p>
      <div class="border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-bg-elevated)] p-3 font-mono text-[11px] space-y-1">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-4 gap-y-1">
          <span class="text-[color:var(--color-text-faint)] uppercase tracking-[0.15em]">Current snapshot</span>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-4 gap-y-1">
          <span class="text-[color:var(--color-text-faint)]">FETCHED</span>
          <span class="text-[color:var(--color-text-bright)]">{fetchedDate}</span>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-4 gap-y-1">
          <span class="text-[color:var(--color-text-faint)]">CSV_SHA256</span>
          <span class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)] break-all">{csvFull}</span>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-4 gap-y-1">
          <span class="text-[color:var(--color-text-faint)]">N_CARDS</span>
          <span class="text-[color:var(--color-text-bright)]">{s.total}</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* Scrape */}
    <section id="scrape" class="border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-bg)]/60 p-5 space-y-3 scroll-mt-20">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-[12px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[color:var(--color-signal-green)]">
        ▸ How we scrape
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
        war.gov sits behind Akamai bot management, which fingerprints the
        TLS handshake and HTTP/2 frame ordering of plain Python clients
        (<code class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">requests</code>,
        <code class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">httpx</code>)
        and serves them a 403. We use
        <code class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">curl_cffi</code>
        with Chrome TLS impersonation, which presents a handshake fingerprint
        identical to a real browser and is allowed through.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
        We deliberately do <em>not</em> use Playwright or any other browser
        automation. The CSV is a static asset; once you can fetch it, the
        scrape stage is a one-shot HTTP GET. Avoiding a browser gives us
        a smaller, more reproducible, and faster pipeline — and removes
        the entire class of "the page changed its DOM" failures.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* OCR */}
    <section id="ocr" class="border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-bg)]/60 p-5 space-y-3 scroll-mt-20">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-[12px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[color:var(--color-signal-green)]">
        ▸ OCR pipeline
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
        Three engines sit behind a common interface. Each page is rasterized
        once and routed to one of:
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-xs font-mono border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-[color:var(--color-border-bright)] text-[color:var(--color-text-faint)] uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-[10px]">
              <th class="text-left py-2 pr-4">Engine</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 pr-4">Hardware</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 pr-4">Strength</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2">Tradeoff</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
            <tr class="border-b border-[color:var(--color-border)]">
              <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">tesseract</td>
              <td class="py-2 pr-4">CPU</td>
              <td class="py-2 pr-4">Fast, free, baseline-quality on clean typewriter scans</td>
              <td class="py-2 text-[color:var(--color-text-dim)]">Struggles on faded / multi-column / handwritten pages</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-[color:var(--color-border)]">
              <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">surya</td>
              <td class="py-2 pr-4">GPU</td>
              <td class="py-2 pr-4">Transformer-based; ~1.87× faster than Tesseract on FBI scans, much better on faded source</td>
              <td class="py-2 text-[color:var(--color-text-dim)]">Requires CUDA hardware; larger model footprint</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">llm (anthropic vision)</td>
              <td class="py-2 pr-4">API</td>
              <td class="py-2 pr-4">Fallback for low-confidence pages; reads the page image directly</td>
              <td class="py-2 text-[color:var(--color-text-dim)]">~$0.10 / 15pp at Sonnet rates; rate-limited; not deterministic</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
        In <code class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">engine="auto"</code>
        mode, the primary engine (Tesseract or Surya) runs first; pages
        whose mean confidence falls below threshold are re-OCR'd by the
        LLM fallback from the original page image — not from the broken
        primary text. The two outputs are independent extractions of the
        same source pixels.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
        Engine and per-page confidence are recorded in
        <code class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">pages.jsonl</code>
        and surfaced in the OCR transcript on each card page. The
        currently-deployed engine for the live search index is documented
        on the (forthcoming) <code class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">/benchmark</code>
        page once the comparison run completes.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* Idempotency */}
    <section id="idempotency" class="border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-bg)]/60 p-5 space-y-3 scroll-mt-20">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-[12px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[color:var(--color-signal-green)]">
        ▸ Idempotency
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
        Every stage — scrape, download, OCR, index — is idempotent against
        a content-hashed manifest. The card identifier is derived from the
        upstream asset URL, with title as a fallback for the rare metadata-only
        row:
      </p>
      <pre class="bg-[color:var(--color-bg-elevated)] border border-[color:var(--color-border)] p-3 font-mono text-[11px] text-[color:var(--color-text-bright)] overflow-x-auto whitespace-pre"><code>card_id = sha256(asset_url || title)[:16]</code></pre>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
        The manifest carries
        <code class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">csv_sha256</code>
        for cheap upstream-change detection. Every stage skips work it has
        already done for unchanged
        <code class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">card_id</code>s.
        Re-running on an unchanged manifest is a no-op modulo timestamps.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
        The manifest itself is shaped like this (one entry per card):
      </p>
      <pre class="bg-[color:var(--color-bg-elevated)] border border-[color:var(--color-border)] p-3 font-mono text-[11px] text-[color:var(--color-text)] overflow-x-auto whitespace-pre"><code>{`{
  "source_url": "https://www.war.gov/.../uap-csv.csv",
  "fetched_at": "2026-05-08T19:21:00Z",
  "csv_sha256": "${csvShort}…",
  "cards": [
    {
      "card_id": "0b298cfc9c65a4d6",
      "title": "NASA-UAP-D6, Apollo 17 Technical Crew Debriefing, 1973",
      "asset_type": "PDF",
      "agency": "NASA",
      "release_date": "...",
      "asset_url": "https://www.war.gov/.../apollo-17.pdf",
      "redacted": false,
      "raw": { ...original CSV row... }
    }
  ]
}`}</code></pre>
    </section>

    {/* What we don't do */}
    <section id="not-do" class="border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-bg)]/60 p-5 space-y-3 scroll-mt-20">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-[12px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[color:var(--color-signal-green)]">
        ▸ What we don't do
      </h2>
      <ul class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)] space-y-2 pl-5 list-disc marker:text-[color:var(--color-text-faint)]">
        <li>
          <span class="text-[color:var(--color-text-bright)]">No data modification.</span>
          The OCR transcript is the engine's literal output. We do not
          spell-check, hand-correct, or "smooth" the text after extraction.
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="text-[color:var(--color-text-bright)]">No transcription guessing.</span>
          When Tesseract produces garbage, the LLM fallback re-OCRs from
          the page image — it is not a "rewrite" of the broken Tesseract
          text. The two engines see the same pixels independently.
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="text-[color:var(--color-text-bright)]">No redaction inference.</span>
          Black bars in the source are preserved as
          <code class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-red)]">[REDACTED]</code>
          tokens. We do not speculate about what was withheld.
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="text-[color:var(--color-text-bright)]">No silent updates.</span>
          When the upstream CSV changes, the diff is captured in the manifest
          history. Every release of this site is reproducible from the
          archived CSV snapshots.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    {/* Limitations */}
    <section id="limitations" class="border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-bg)]/60 p-5 space-y-3 scroll-mt-20">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-[12px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[color:var(--color-signal-green)]">
        ▸ Limitations
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
        Honest framing — these are the failure modes you should expect
        when reading OCR output from this corpus:
      </p>
      <ul class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)] space-y-2 pl-5 list-disc marker:text-[color:var(--color-text-faint)]">
        <li>
          <span class="text-[color:var(--color-text-bright)]">Faded scans.</span>
          The 1947–1968 FBI material varies wildly in scan quality. Tesseract
          frequently produces gibberish on the worst pages; Surya and the
          LLM fallback do better but are not perfect.
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="text-[color:var(--color-text-bright)]">Reading order.</span>
          Multi-column forms — particularly the carbon-copy intake forms
          common in the FBI files — occasionally lose reading order. Column
          detection is heuristic.
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="text-[color:var(--color-text-bright)]">Redaction boundaries.</span>
          When a redaction crosses a word boundary or sits inline with
          surrounding text, the OCR output may lose adjacent characters.
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="text-[color:var(--color-text-bright)]">Handwriting.</span>
          Marginalia and signatures are inconsistently captured. Surya
          handles these better than Tesseract; the LLM fallback handles
          them best but at API cost.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
        A published quality benchmark — engine-by-engine, with golden-set
        WER and CER numbers — will live at
        <code class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">/benchmark</code>
        once the comparison run completes.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* License */}
    <section id="license" class="border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-bg)]/60 p-5 space-y-3 scroll-mt-20">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-[12px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[color:var(--color-signal-green)]">
        ▸ License
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
        The source documents are works of the U.S. Government and are in
        the public domain. The OCR transcripts derived from them carry no
        additional copyright claim from us — they are mechanically generated
        from public-domain originals.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
        The index code and this site are
        <span class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">© BPS AI Software</span>.
        License: TBD before public launch.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* Reproducibility */}
    <section id="reproducibility" class="border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-bg)]/60 p-5 space-y-3 scroll-mt-20">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-[12px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[color:var(--color-signal-green)]">
        ▸ Reproducibility
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
        The full pipeline lives at
        <a href="https://github.com/BPSAI/pursue-index" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)] underline decoration-[color:var(--color-border-bright)] hover:decoration-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">github.com/BPSAI/pursue-index</a>.
        The manifest is version-controlled in the repo, hash-pinned to the
        upstream CSV. From a clean clone with the upstream CSV available,
        any reader can:
      </p>
      <pre class="bg-[color:var(--color-bg-elevated)] border border-[color:var(--color-border)] p-3 font-mono text-[11px] text-[color:var(--color-text-bright)] overflow-x-auto whitespace-pre"><code>{`pursue scrape run    # writes manifests/latest.json + archives raw CSV
pursue download run  # fetches PDFs/IMGs idempotently
pursue ocr run       # writes pages.jsonl per card
pursue index ingest  # builds the search payload`}</code></pre>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
        Every stage is rerunnable on the same manifest as a no-op. Every
        stage's output is content-addressed by
        <code class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">card_id</code>,
        so partial reruns converge on the same final state regardless of order.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* Benchmark placeholder */}
    <section id="benchmark" class="border border-[color:var(--color-signal-amber)]/40 bg-[color:var(--color-signal-amber)]/5 p-5 space-y-2 scroll-mt-20">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-[12px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[color:var(--color-signal-amber)]">
        ▸ Quality benchmark — pending
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
        A&nbsp;published A/B benchmark across Tesseract, Surya, and the LLM
        fallback — over a golden test set drawn from this corpus — will
        live at <code class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">/benchmark</code>
        when the comparison run completes. Expect WER, CER, per-engine
        wall-clock, and the threshold at which the fallback fires.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-[color:var(--color-text-faint)] pt-2">
      DOC · <span class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-amber)]">RESEARCH PREVIEW</span>
      <span class="mx-2">·</span>
      REV · <span class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">0.2</span>
      <span class="mx-2">·</span>
      CSV · <span class="text-[color:var(--color-signal-cyan)]">{csvShort}</span>
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
